{"q":{"id":"Q26","question":"Where may a new Spy be placed, what if all your Spies are already deployed, and what if its placement effect specifies an Agent-icon type?"},"key":{"id":"Q26","expected_answer":"Unoccupied observation post, from supply. If none in supply may first recall one of own Spies for no effect. Specified icon must connect to post or cannot place there.","source_references":"Main p11 / extraction 588–605","novelty":"Spy placement and exhaustion"},"A":{"id":"Q26","answer":"Place a Spy from your supply on an unoccupied observation post. If you have none in supply when placement is needed, you may first recall one of your Spies for no effect. If the placement effect specifies a required connected Agent-icon type, the Spy may be placed only at a post connected to that icon; otherwise placement is not legal.","support":"supported","evidence":"Special Rules: Spies & observation posts"},"B":{"id":"Q26","answer":"Place a Spy from supply on an unoccupied observation post; if none are in supply, you may first recall one of your Spies for no effect. If placement specifies an Agent-icon type, the post must connect to a space with that icon or the placement is not legal.","support":"supported","evidence":"Special Rules: Spies & observation posts."}}
{"q":{"id":"Q27","question":"Can you Infiltrate your own occupied board space, use a Spy without playing a card, or recall two connected Spies to Gather Intelligence twice on one placement?"},"key":{"id":"Q27","expected_answer":"No to all. Infiltrate bypasses another player Agent; still play a card and meet entry costs/requirements. Two Spies can pay different effects (Infiltrate and Gather Intelligence), not duplicate Gather Intelligence.","source_references":"Main p11; FAQ p4 Spies","novelty":"Spy ownership and per-effect recall limits"},"A":{"id":"Q27","answer":"No: Infiltrate cannot bypass your own Agent. A Spy does not allow an Agent turn without playing a card and meeting its normal icon access condition. No: two Spies cannot be recalled to get two Gather Intelligence draws on one placement; at most one draw is allowed.","support":"supported","evidence":"Special Rules: Infiltrate; Gather Intelligence; FAQ Spies"},"B":{"id":"Q27","answer":"No, Infiltrate cannot bypass your own Agent. A Spy does not give a free Agent turn: play a card and meet its normal access condition. No, two Spies cannot each provide Gather Intelligence on one placement; at most one draw is allowed.","support":"supported","evidence":"Special Rules: Infiltrate; Gather Intelligence; Spy Agent access."}}
{"q":{"id":"Q28","question":"Who gets control bonuses when visiting Arrakeen, Spice Refinery or Imperial Basin, how is control transferred, and where does the defensive troop originate?"},"key":{"id":"Q28","expected_answer":"Winning respective Conflict puts control marker on flag replacing opponent. Controller receives 1 Solari at Arrakeen/Refinery or 1 spice at Basin whenever anyone including self visits. Reveal of controlled location Conflict permits one troop from supply directly to Conflict.","source_references":"Main p10,p20 / extraction 491–506,1099–1103","novelty":"Control transfer, repeated benefit and defense"},"A":{"id":"Q28","answer":"At Arrakeen, Spice Refinery and Imperial Basin, the controller gains the location bonus whenever any player visits, including the controller themself. Winning the corresponding Conflict places/replaces a Control marker. If the Conflict is revealed for a location you already control, you may deploy one troop from your supply directly to the Conflict, without an Agent visit. Control may transfer when another player wins that location Conflict.","support":"supported","evidence":"Critical Locations & Control"},"B":{"id":"Q28","answer":"The controller gets its separate bonus whenever any player, including itself, visits: 1 Solari at Arrakeen or Spice Refinery, 1 spice at Imperial Basin. Winning the named Conflict places/replaces control. When a controlled Conflict is revealed, the controller may deploy 1 troop from supply directly to Conflict without visiting.","support":"supported","evidence":"Special Rules: Critical Locations & Control."}}
{"q":{"id":"Q29","question":"When two players tie for second in a three-player Conflict, what happens? What if players tie for third in four-player play, or three players tie for first?"},"key":{"id":"Q29","expected_answer":"Tied second each get third reward; winner keeps first and card, no other rewards. Tied third receive nothing. Three tied first each get second, no winner/card and no further rewards.","source_references":"Main p14 / extraction 792–808","novelty":"Remaining combat tie outcome branches"},"A":{"id":"Q29","answer":"A tie for second gives each tied player the third reward; the Conflict winner keeps first reward and card, with no further rewards. A tie for third receives nothing. Three tied for first each take second reward; nobody else receives rewards and nobody takes the card.","support":"supported","evidence":"Combat ties by player count"},"B":{"id":"Q29","answer":"Tie for second: each tied player gets third reward; winner keeps first reward and card; no further rewards. Tie for third: tied players get nothing. Three tied first: each gets second reward, nobody takes the card, and no other rewards, in either three- or four-player play.","support":"supported","evidence":"Special Rules: Combat ties; Three tied for first."}}
{"q":{"id":"Q30","question":"At a normal three-player Conflict with three different positive strengths, which places receive rewards? What if a two-player game’s Rival is one of the participants?"},"key":{"id":"Q30","expected_answer":"Only first and second rewarded, third only used for a tie for second. Two humans plus standard Rival use the same two-/three-player reward rules; Rival competes normally.","source_references":"Main p14; Supplements p5 Combat; FAQ p1 Conflict","novelty":"Untied low-player-count rewards"},"A":{"id":"Q30","answer":"In an ordinary three-player Conflict with distinct positive strengths, first and second place receive their rewards; third place receives nothing. This is also the reward rule when a two-player game’s Rival is the third competitor.","support":"supported","evidence":"Resolve Combat: player count determines which ranks are paid"},"B":{"id":"Q30","answer":"In a three-player Conflict with three distinct positive strengths, first and second get their rewards; third gets none. The same ordinary rank rule applies to two humans plus the Rival: first and second receive rewards, untied third does not.","support":"supported","evidence":"Phase 3: Resolve Combat; Player count determines ranks."}}

