{"q":{"id":"Q46","question":"State Accept Contract and Dutiful Service’s Agent icons and effects with and without CHOAM, and whether both automatically advance a Faction track."},"key":{"id":"Q46","expected_answer":"Accept Contract uses the Spice Trade Agent icon: draw one card and resolve a contract icon; it does not automatically gain Faction Influence. Dutiful Service uses the Emperor Agent icon: gain one Emperor Influence and resolve a contract icon. Without CHOAM each contract icon gives 2 Solari. With CHOAM, take an available public face-up contract into your supply and refill from the bank if possible; the icon gives 2 Solari if no such contract remains. For another human, Shaddam's unacquired set-aside Sardaukar contracts are unavailable and do not prevent this fallback when the public supply is empty. Shaddam's reserved-contract acquisition option remains governed by his Leader and the FAQ.","source_references":"See benchmark-v17/source-reference-appendix.json: original main PDF pp16,20, Uprising Board Space Guide, original Shaddam Leader and official FAQ p3, exact named-file/hash locators.","novelty":"Nonfaction versus Faction contract spaces"},"A":{"id":"Q46","answer":"Accept Contract uses Spice Trade access; it costs nothing, always draws one card, and resolves the contract icon: without CHOAM gain 2 Solari; with CHOAM take a public face-up contract and refill if possible, or gain 2 Solari if offers are exhausted. It gives no automatic Faction Influence and is not Combat. Dutiful Service uses Emperor access, costs nothing, gains one Emperor Influence (counted once) and resolves the same contract icon; it is not Combat. It advances Emperor automatically, unlike Accept Contract.","support":"supported","evidence":"Board Space Guide; human contract icon rule; Faction status"},"B":{"id":"Q46","answer":"Accept Contract is Spice Trade, no cost, no Combat: always draw 1 card; without CHOAM contract icon gives 2 Solari, with CHOAM take/refill public contract or get 2 Solari if offers exhausted. It is non-Faction, no automatic Influence. Dutiful Service is Emperor, no cost, no Combat: gain 1 Emperor Influence plus the same contract-icon effect; the listed gain and automatic Faction advance are one Influence total, not two.","support":"supported","evidence":"Board Space Guide; Faction status of contract spaces."}}
{"q":{"id":"Q47","question":"What do Arrakeen, Fremkit and Research Station each cost and provide, what Agent icons access them, and which are Combat spaces?"},"key":{"id":"Q47","expected_answer":"Arrakeen City free, troop and card, controller 1 Solari. Fremkit Fremen free, 1 Fremen Influence and card. Research Station City 2 water, two troops and two cards. All Combat.","source_references":"Supplements pp1–2","novelty":"Free versus paid card/recruit Combat spaces"},"A":{"id":"Q47","answer":"Arrakeen: City icon, no cost, Combat yes; visitor recruits one troop and draws one card, controller gains 1 Solari. Fremkit: Fremen icon, no cost, Combat yes; gain 1 Fremen Influence and draw a card. Research Station: City icon, costs 2 water, Combat yes; recruit two troops and draw two cards.","support":"supported","evidence":"Board Space Guide"},"B":{"id":"Q47","answer":"Arrakeen: City, no cost, Combat yes; visitor recruits 1 troop and draws 1 card, controller gains 1 Solari. Fremkit: Fremen, no cost, Combat yes; gain 1 Fremen Influence and draw 1 card. Research Station: City, 2 water, Combat yes; recruit 2 troops and draw 2 cards.","support":"supported","evidence":"Board Space Guide."}}
{"q":{"id":"Q48","question":"Compare Deep Desert and Hagga Basin’s entry costs, base spice and worm choices. Do you still collect bonus spice when choosing worms, and are Hooks spent?"},"key":{"id":"Q48","expected_answer":"Deep Desert 3 water, 4 spice OR two worms; Hagga 1 water, 2 spice OR one worm. Both collect all bonus spice even with worms. Worm choice requires Maker Hooks, not spent; cannot summon into Shield-Wall-protected Conflict.","source_references":"Supplements pp1–2; Main p10; FAQ p2 Maker hooks","novelty":"Maker choice excludes base spice but retains bonus"},"A":{"id":"Q48","answer":"Deep Desert uses Spice Trade access, costs 3 water, and offers either 4 base spice or, with Maker Hooks, two sandworms. Hagga Basin also uses Spice Trade, costs 1 water, and offers either 2 base spice or, with Hooks, one sandworm. Both grant all accumulated bonus spice regardless of choosing worms; Hooks are retained, not spent.","support":"supported","evidence":"Board Space Guide; Sandworms; FAQ Spies/Hooks"},"B":{"id":"Q48","answer":"Deep Desert costs 3 water, base choice 4 spice or (with Maker Hooks) 2 sandworms. Hagga Basin costs 1 water, base choice 2 spice or (with Hooks) 1 sandworm. Both also grant all bonus spice even when choosing worms. Hooks are not spent; effects specifying Hooks require having the token.","support":"supported","evidence":"Board Space Guide; Sandworms."}}
{"q":{"id":"Q49","question":"For each Sietch Tabr option, state requirements and effects. Do you get a troop when choosing to remove the Shield Wall?"},"key":{"id":"Q49","expected_answer":"City access, 2+ Fremen Influence, no resource cost. Option one Hooks if needed, one troop, one water; option two one water and may remove wall. Option two gives no troop or Hooks. Combat space.","source_references":"Supplements p2","novelty":"Sietch mutually exclusive option branches"},"A":{"id":"Q49","answer":"Both Sietch Tabr options require City access and 2+ Fremen Influence, with no resource entry cost. Option one: take Maker Hooks if you lack them, recruit one troop, gain 1 water. Option two: gain 1 water and optionally remove the Shield Wall. The second option gives no troop.","support":"supported","evidence":"Board Space Guide"},"B":{"id":"Q49","answer":"Both options require City access and 2+ Fremen Influence, with no resource cost. Option 1: take Maker Hooks if you lack them, recruit 1 troop, gain 1 water. Option 2: gain 1 water and optionally remove Shield Wall; it gives no troop.","support":"supported","evidence":"Board Space Guide: Sietch Tabr."}}
{"q":{"id":"Q50","question":"How do High Council’s first and subsequent visits differ, what does each cost, when is its ongoing benefit used, and is it a Combat space?"},"key":{"id":"Q50","expected_answer":"Landsraad 5 Solari each visit. First Councilor to empty seat, +2 Persuasion each Reveal rest of game; later 2 spice, Intrigue, three troops. Not Combat, cannot normally deploy recruited troops.","source_references":"Supplements p2","novelty":"High Council persistent upgrade versus revisit rewards"},"A":{"id":"Q50","answer":"High Council uses Landsraad access and costs 5 Solari each visit; it is not Combat. First visit: place Councilor on an unoccupied seat and gain 2 Persuasion on every Reveal turn for the rest of the game. Subsequent visits: gain 2 spice, draw an Intrigue and recruit three troops.","support":"supported","evidence":"Board Space Guide"},"B":{"id":"Q50","answer":"High Council is Landsraad, no Combat, costs 5 Solari each visit. First visit puts your Councilor on an unoccupied seat and grants 2 Persuasion on every Reveal turn thereafter. Later visits grant 2 spice, draw an Intrigue, and recruit 3 troops.","support":"supported","evidence":"Board Space Guide: High Council."}}

